\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 This is the climactic theological statement of the entire NT on the Father-Son order.
\f2\b0 \
Let us read it slowly, in the Greek:\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u8005 \u964 \u945 \u957  \u948 \u8050  \u8017 \u960 \u959 \u964 \u945 \u947 \u8135  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183  \u964 \u8048  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "when all things shall have been subjected to Him [the Son]"
\f2\i0 \
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u964 \u972 \u964 \u949 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "then"
\f2\i0  \'97 at that precise moment in the divine order\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8056 \u962  \u8001  \u933 \u7985 \u8056 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "even the Son Himself"
\f2\i0  \'97 the emphatic \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u972 \u962  underscores that 
\f0\b even He, even the Son
\f2\b0  \'97 yes, even the Son Himself\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u8017 \u960 \u959 \u964 \u945 \u947 \u942 \u963 \u949 \u964 \u945 \u953 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "shall be subjected"
\f2\i0  \'97 
\f0\b future passive
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i He will be made subject
\f2\i0 \
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u964 \u8183  \u8017 \u960 \u959 \u964 \u940 \u958 \u945 \u957 \u964 \u953  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183  \u964 \u8048  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "to Him who subjected all things to Him"
\f2\i0  \'97 that is, 
\f0\b to the Father
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b Paul writes, by inspiration, that at the consummation of all things, the Son Himself will be subjected to the Father.
\f2\b0 \
The Geneva captures it without softening: 
\f1\i "Then shall the Son also himself be subject unto him, that did subdue all things under him."
