\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Honest theological assessment per our maxims\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Per maxim #1 \'97 
\f0\b let God be true
\f2\b0  \'97 we must take this verse at face value. Paul, writing under inspiration, declares:\
- 
\f0\b The Son delivers the Kingdom to the Father.
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b The Son Himself is subjected to the Father.
\f2\b0 \
This is not a metaphorical or rhetorical subjection. The Greek 
\f0\b \uc0\u8017 \u960 \u959 \u964 \u945 \u947 \u942 \u963 \u949 \u964 \u945 \u953 
\f2\b0  is the same verb used of all created things being subjected \'97 only here applied to the Son's own willing, eternal posture before the Father.\
This text 
\f0\b rules out any reading
\f2\b0  that says:\
- The Son is 
\f0\b co-equal
\f2\b0  with the Father in such a way that He never submits.\
- The Son and Father are 
\f0\b interchangeable Persons
\f2\b0  of one substance with no order of authority between them.\
- The Son's exaltation 
\f0\b erases His distinction from and subordination to
\f2\b0  the Father.\
Per maxim #3, this is precisely the kind of text that the post-Nicene formulations had to navigate around with subtle distinctions ("subordination in role but not in essence," etc.). 
\f0\b Paul does not make such distinctions.
\f2\b0  He says plainly: 
\f0\b the Son will be subjected to the Father.
\f2\b0 \
What this 
\f0\b does
\f2\b0  preserve perfectly is what we have established throughout our study:\
- The Father is the One supreme God.\
- The Son is the Father's Beloved, Begotten, Sent, Anointed, Exalted One.\
- The Son's reign \'97 even His everlasting reign \'97 is 
\f0\b under and unto the Father's supreme authority
\f2\b0 .\
- The Son delights to subject Himself to the Father, because 
\f0\b He always does what pleases the Father
\f2\b0  (John 8:29).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

