\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 (2) The Son's reign is real, glorious, and eternal \'97 but always derivative and unto the Father's glory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - He receives the Kingdom from the Ancient of Days (Dan 7:14).\
- He reigns with all authority given Him (Matt 28:18).\
- He hands the Kingdom back to the Father at the end (1 Cor 15:24).\
- His eternal subjection to the Father is not loss; it is the perfection of the Son-Father relation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 (3) The throne in the eternal state is jointly held by the Father and the Lamb.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - 
\f0\b "The throne of God and of the Lamb"
\f2\b0  (Rev 22:1, 3) \'97 singular throne, shared by two.\
- The river of life proceeds from this joint throne.\
- The redeemed serve them together as one \'97 but the Father is the One whose face is ultimately seen, and the Lamb is the means by which His face is revealed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 (4) The Lamb retains His humanity forever.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - He is 
\f0\b the Lamb
\f2\b0  \'97 the slain and risen Man Christ Jesus.\
- The pierced One bears the marks eternally (cf. Zech 13:6; John 20:25-27).\
- The Mediator between God and men is 
\f0\b the Man
\f2\b0  Christ Jesus (1 Tim 2:5) \'97 eternally.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 (5) The pattern from Genesis to the consummation is unbroken:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The Father is the Source; the Son is the Sent; all things move toward the Father's glory.
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b Creation:
\f2\b0  the Father creates 
\f0\b through
\f2\b0  the Son (1 Cor 8:6; Col 1:16; Heb 1:2; John 1:3).\
- 
\f0\b Incarnation:
\f2\b0  the Father prepares a body for the Son; the Son comes to do the Father's will (Heb 10:5-7).\
- 
\f0\b Death:
\f2\b0  the Father delivers up the Son for our sins (Isa 53:6, 10; Rom 8:32).\
- 
\f0\b Resurrection:
\f2\b0  the Father raises the Son and declares Him Son of God in power (Acts 13:33; Rom 1:4).\
- 
\f0\b Exaltation:
\f2\b0  the Father seats the Son at His right hand and gives Him the Name above every name (Phil 2:9-11; Acts 2:36).\
- 
\f0\b Reign:
\f2\b0  the Son reigns by the authority given Him until all enemies are subdued (1 Cor 15:25; Ps 110:1).\
- 
\f0\b Return:
\f2\b0  the Son comes at the Father's appointed time (Mark 13:32) to judge by the Father's grant (John 5:27).\
- 
\f0\b Consummation:
\f2\b0  the Son delivers the Kingdom to the Father and is Himself subjected to Him, that God may be all in all (1 Cor 15:24-28).\

\f0\b At every stage, the Father is the Originator, the Sender, the Appointer, the Giver, the One whose will is done, the One whose glory is the goal. And the Son is the willing, beloved, obedient, glorified Sent One \'97 never a rival, never an equal in such a way as to displace the Father's supremacy.
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 A Concluding Theological Observation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The doctrine of 
\f0\b eternal subordination of the Son to the Father
\f2\b0  has been a contested point in church history. The post-Nicene party \'97 and especially the later Western tradition \'97 tended to insist that any "subordination" was 
\f1\i only
\f2\i0  in the economy of salvation (His earthly mission), not in His eternal relation. But 
\f0\b Paul's text in 1 Cor 15:28 cannot bear that distinction.
\f2\b0  Paul says explicitly: 
\f1\i "when all things shall be subdued unto Him, 
\f3\b then
\f1\b0  shall the Son also himself be subject unto Him that did subdue all things under Him."
\f2\i0 \
The subjection is 
\f0\b future
\f2\b0  (
\f0\b \uc0\u8017 \u960 \u959 \u964 \u945 \u947 \u942 \u963 \u949 \u964 \u945 \u953 
\f2\b0 ). It occurs 
\f0\b after
\f2\b0  the present reign is complete. It is the 
\f0\b eternal posture
\f2\b0  of the Son in the consummation. The Father is 
\f0\b eternally
\f2\b0  the supreme One God; the Son is 
\f0\b eternally
\f2\b0  the beloved Son who delights to be subject to His Father.\
Per our maxim #3, we do not need to soften this with post-Nicene metaphysical formulations. 
\f0\b Paul speaks plainly, and we let his words stand.
\f2\b0 \
This does not diminish the Son. It is His glory and His joy. He has said throughout His ministry: 
\f1\i "I do always those things that please him"
\f2\i0  (John 8:29, 1599 Geneva). His eternal subjection to the Father is the eternal perfection of that filial obedience and love.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 What Remains\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We have now completed (A), (B), and (C). What remains:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

