\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 REVELATION 1:8 \'97 A CRITICAL TEXT REQUIRING CAREFUL EXAMINATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the ending, saith the Lord, Which is, and Which was, and Which is to come, even the Almighty.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Honest examination \'97 who is speaking?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 This verse is crucial because it is often cited as evidence that Jesus is Alpha and Omega. Let us read it carefully.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 The speaker identifies Himself as:
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u964 \u8056  \u7948 \u955 \u966 \u945  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u964 \u8056  \u8046 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the Alpha and the Omega"
\f2\i0  (the first and the last letters of the Greek alphabet \'97 meaning 
\f1\i "the First and the Last"
\f2\i0 )\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u922 \u973 \u961 \u953 \u959 \u962  \u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the Lord God"
\f2\i0  \'97 combining the divine titles\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u8034 \u957  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u7974 \u957  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u7952 \u961 \u967 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u957 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the One who is, was, and is to come"
\f2\i0  \'97 
\f0\b the exact title of the Father given in v. 4
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u928 \u945 \u957 \u964 \u959 \u954 \u961 \u940 \u964 \u969 \u961 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the Almighty / All-Sovereign"
\f2\i0  \'97 the LXX standard rendering of 
\f0\b YHWH Sabaoth
\f2\b0 , the LORD of Hosts\

\f0\b Honest assessment:
\f2\b0  The titles in v. 8 are 
\f0\b identical to those given to the Father in v. 4
\f2\b0 , especially 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u8034 \u957  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u7974 \u957  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u7952 \u961 \u967 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u957 \u959 \u962 .
\f2\b0  The most natural reading is that 
\f0\b the Father Himself is speaking in v. 8
\f2\b0  \'97 interrupting briefly to declare His own eternal identity, before the narrative continues with John's vision.\
Some interpreters take this as Jesus speaking (because Jesus also uses 
\f0\b "Alpha and Omega"
\f2\b0  in Rev 22:13). But:\
- 
\f0\b v. 4
\f2\b0  has just identified 
\f0\b "the One who is, was, and is to come"
\f2\b0  as distinct from 
\f0\b Jesus Christ
\f2\b0  (named after "Him").\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u928 \u945 \u957 \u964 \u959 \u954 \u961 \u940 \u964 \u969 \u961 
\f2\b0  is the LXX title for 
\f0\b YHWH Sabaoth
\f2\b0  \'97 the Father.\
- Throughout Revelation, 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u928 \u945 \u957 \u964 \u959 \u954 \u961 \u940 \u964 \u969 \u961 
\f2\b0  is consistently used of the Father (Rev 4:8; 11:17; 15:3; 16:7; 19:6; 21:22).\

\f0\b Therefore, by careful reading, v. 8 is the Father speaking.
\f2\b0  This is the Father's self-identification as the Alpha and Omega, the Almighty, the One who eternally is, was, and is to come.\
This 
\f0\b does not preclude
\f2\b0  the Son from also being called "Alpha and Omega" in Rev 22:13 \'97 but we will examine that text carefully on its own when we reach it, and we will not pre-commit to identification.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
