\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Examination of who speaks vv. 12-13\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 With our newly-articulated typological lens, the question becomes clearer.
\f2\b0 \
The speaker in v. 12 says 
\f0\b "I am coming quickly"
\f2\b0  \'97 and v. 16 begins 
\f0\b "I Jesus have sent My angel."
\f2\b0  The natural reading is that 
\f0\b Jesus is the speaker
\f2\b0  in vv. 12-16. The "coming quickly" language is His (Rev 22:7, 20), and the self-identification as 
\f0\b "the Root and the Offspring of David, the bright Morning Star"
\f2\b0  in v. 16 is unmistakably Jesus.\
Therefore: 
\f0\b Jesus is the speaker in v. 13
\f2\b0  \'97 declaring Himself 
\f0\b "the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End."
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b Per your typological observation, this is wholly true to type:
\f2\b0 \
- Jesus is 
\f0\b the Alpha and Omega of the Father's redemptive economy
\f2\b0  \'97 He is the One through whom all things were made (John 1:3; Col 1:16) and unto whom all things will be gathered (Eph 1:10), the firstborn over creation and the firstborn from the dead (Col 1:15, 18), the One who initiates and consummates the Father's saving purpose. The redemptive arc begins and ends in Him \'97 as the Father's Only-Begotten.\
- This 
\f0\b does not displace
\f2\b0  the Father, who in Rev 21:5-7 declares 
\f0\b the same title
\f2\b0  of Himself in His absolute, original sense \'97 as the One God who originates and consummates all being.\
- 
\f0\b The Son's "Alpha and Omega" is the manifestation of the Father's "Alpha and Omega" in the Person of the Only-Begotten.
\f2\b0 \
This is the same pattern we have noted throughout:\
- The Father stretches out the heavens 
\f0\b alone
\f2\b0  (Isa 44:24); the Father creates 
\f0\b through
\f2\b0  the Son (1 Cor 8:6) \'97 the work is one act, the Father acting through the Son who is His own Word and Image.\
- The Father is the Saviour (Isa 45:21); the Son is the Saviour (Matt 1:21) \'97 because salvation is one work, accomplished by the Father through the Son.\
- The Father is the First and the Last (Isa 44:6); the Son is the First and the Last (Rev 1:17; 22:13) \'97 because the Son is the Father's full self-expression to the creation.\

\f0\b True to type, as you have rightly said.
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
