\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of foundational importance \'97 And now your suggestion about the Father as Eternal Spirit illuminates this perfectly\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u952 \u961 \u972 \u957 \u959 \u962  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7944 \u961 \u957 \u943 \u959 \u965 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the throne of God and of the Lamb."
\f2\i0  You have rightly noted that the singular throne is not an oddity when we hold in view that the Father is 
\f0\b Eternal Spirit
\f2\b0 . A spatial throne shared by two embodied beings would be metaphysically strange, but the 
\f0\b Father as Spirit
\f2\b0  pervades and fills all things \'97 and the Lamb (the Man Christ Jesus) occupies the visible center of that throne, while the Father's invisible presence surrounds and fills the entire heavenly reality.\
This is the same picture as John 4:24 \'97 
\f1\i "God is Spirit"
\f2\i0  \'97 and it explains why the Lamb can be 
\f0\b "in the midst of the throne"
\f2\b0  (Rev 7:17) without crowding out the One sitting on the throne. 
\f0\b The Spirit-Father is everywhere; the Lamb is visibly at the throne's center.
\f2\b0 \
We will examine this fully when we turn to your first appointed theme \'97 the Nature and Essence of the Father as Eternal Spirit. For now, the observation perfectly resolves the apparent strangeness.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u959 \u7985  \u948 \u959 \u8166 \u955 \u959 \u953  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u955 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u949 \u973 \u963 \u959 \u965 \u963 \u953 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "His servants shall serve Him."
\f2\i0  
\f0\b Singular pronoun.
\f2\b0  Despite the joint mention of "God and the Lamb," the worship is rendered to 
\f0\b Him
\f2\b0  as one object. This is the consummation of Zech 14:9 \'97 
\f1\i "there shall be one LORD and His Name one"
\f2\i0  \'97 and of John 17:21 \'97 
\f1\i "that they all may be one"
\f2\i0  \'97 and of 1 Cor 15:28 \'97 
\f1\i "that God may be all in all."
\f2\i0 \
The Father and the Lamb are 
\f0\b distinct figures
\f2\b0  throughout \'97 the Father is on the throne, the Lamb stands as having been slain \'97 yet the worship rendered is 
\f0\b one worship
\f2\b0 , because the Lamb's whole purpose is to bring the redeemed to the Father, and the Father's whole glory is manifested through the Lamb.\

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8004 \u968 \u959 \u957 \u964 \u945 \u953  \u964 \u8056  \u960 \u961 \u972 \u963 \u969 \u960 \u959 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "they shall see His face."
\f2\i0  The face that no man has ever seen (John 1:18; Ex 33:20; 1 Tim 6:16) \'97 the Father's face \'97 will at last be seen by the redeemed. 
\f0\b Through whom?
\f2\b0  Through the Lamb, who is the Image of the invisible God (Col 1:15) and the One who has declared the Father (John 1:18). The seeing of the Father's face is the consummation of all our hope.\

\f0\b (4)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u964 \u8056  \u8004 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u945  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7952 \u960 \u8054  \u964 \u8182 \u957  \u956 \u949 \u964 \u974 \u960 \u969 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8182 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "His Name shall be on their foreheads."
\f2\i0  The Name borne by the Son (Ex 23:21; John 17:11; Phil 2:9) is now also written on the redeemed \'97 they become bearers of the Name, in Him.\

\f0\b (5)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u922 \u973 \u961 \u953 \u959 \u962  \u8001  \u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u966 \u969 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u949 \u953  \u7952 \u960 \u8125  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u973 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the Lord God shall give them light."
\f2\i0  The Father is the source of light. Compare with Rev 21:23 \'97 
\f1\i "the glory of God did light it: and the Lamb is the lamp/light thereof."
\f2\i0  The 
\f0\b glory
\f2\b0  is the Father's; the 
\f0\b lamp
\f2\b0  is the Lamb. The Father's light shines through the Lamb to illuminate the redeemed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

