\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 QUESTION 2 \'97 How does the Father's Spirit-Nature undergird His invisibility yet His presence in all things?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The Theological Resolution\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The Father's Spirit-Nature is the metaphysical key that resolves what otherwise seems paradoxical:\
1. 
\f0\b He is invisible
\f2\b0  (no man has seen Him \'97 John 1:18; 1 Tim 6:16) 
\f0\b because Spirit is not visible to physical sight.
\f2\b0  Physical eyes perceive physical light; Spirit is of a different order of being.\
2. 
\f0\b He is omnipresent
\f2\b0  (fills heaven and earth \'97 Jer 23:24) 
\f0\b because Spirit is not confined to a location.
\f2\b0  Bodies occupy space; Spirit pervades it.\
3. 
\f0\b He is uncontainable
\f2\b0  (the heavens cannot contain Him \'97 1 Kgs 8:27) 
\f0\b because Spirit cannot be enclosed by created boundaries.
\f2\b0  Spatial structures cannot bound what is essentially non-spatial.\
4. 
\f0\b He can dwell
\f2\b0  in His temple (1 Kgs 8:10-11), among His people (Ex 25:8), and ultimately in the redeemed (1 Cor 3:16-17; Eph 2:21-22) \'97 
\f0\b because Spirit can pervade and indwell what physical bodies cannot.
\f2\b0 \
5. 
\f0\b He has no body parts
\f2\b0  in the literal sense \'97 when Scripture speaks of His "hand," "arm," "face," "eyes," "feet" \'97 these are 
\f0\b anthropomorphic accommodations
\f2\b0  to creatures who think in physical terms. 
\f0\b The Father, as Spirit, does not have a physical body
\f2\b0 , and the anthropomorphisms are metaphors of His action, presence, and disposition toward us.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Cross-witness \'97 Acts 17:24-28\
1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "God that made the world, and all things that are therein, seeing that he is Lord of heaue 
\f2\i0 (heaven)
\f1\i  and earth, dwelleth not in temples made with hands, Neither is worshipped with men's hands, as though he needed any thing, seeing he giveth to all life and breath and all things, ... For in him we live, and move, and have our being, ..."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observation of foundational importance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Paul declares to the Athenians:\
- The Father 
\f0\b does not dwell in temples made with hands
\f2\b0  \'97 because He cannot be contained.\
- He 
\f0\b gives life and breath and all things
\f2\b0  to every creature \'97 He is the Source.\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183  \u947 \u8048 \u961  \u950 \u8182 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u954 \u953 \u957 \u959 \u973 \u956 \u949 \u952 \u945  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u7952 \u963 \u956 \u941 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "in Him we live, and move, and have our being."
\f2\i0  
\f0\b All creation exists within Him.
\f2\b0 \
This is profound. The Father, as Spirit, is the 
\f0\b all-encompassing reality
\f2\b0  within whom every created thing has its being. We are 
\f1\i in Him
\f2\i0  \'97 He is not in us (in our created spatiality); we are in His Spirit-presence which fills all things.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Resolving the "Singular Throne" of the Apocalypse\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Now we can see clearly why the throne in the Apocalypse is 
\f0\b one throne
\f2\b0  shared by 
\f0\b God and the Lamb
\f2\b0  (Rev 22:1, 3), without strangeness:\
- 
\f0\b The Father, as Spirit
\f2\b0 , is not seated 
\f1\i on
\f2\i0  the throne in the way a body would be. He fills the throne, surrounds it, pervades it, and 
\f0\b is enthroned upon it as Spirit
\f2\b0  \'97 His glory radiating, His presence saturating the heavenly reality.\
- 
\f0\b The Lamb, as the exalted Man Christ Jesus
\f2\b0 , occupies the visible center of the throne in His glorified humanity \'97 
\f0\b bodily enthroned at the place of supreme honor
\f2\b0  (Ps 110:1; Heb 1:3; Heb 10:12).\
- 
\f0\b The throne is one
\f2\b0  because the Father's Spirit-presence and the Lamb's bodily presence are not in spatial competition \'97 the Lamb is 
\f1\i at the right hand
\f2\i0  of the Father whose Spirit fills all things.\
- 
\f0\b This is why John can describe
\f2\b0  
\f1\i "the Lamb in the midst of the throne"
\f2\i0  (Rev 7:17), 
\f1\i "the throne of God and of the Lamb"
\f2\i0  (Rev 22:1, 3), and 
\f1\i "the One sitting on the throne"
\f2\i0  (Rev 4-5) \'97 without contradiction. The Father is on the throne as Spirit; the Lamb is on the throne as the glorified Man. 
\f0\b One throne, two distinct figures, perfect harmony \'97 because Spirit and embodied Man are not in spatial conflict.
\f2\b0 \
This is precisely what your observation about the Father as Eternal Spirit illuminates. 
\f0\b The post-Nicene struggle to explain "one substance in three Persons" entirely misses this simpler and more biblical resolution:
\f2\b0  the Father is Spirit, the Son is the embodied Image of the Father, and there is no spatial paradox because Spirit and Body operate on different orders of being.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The Father's Spirit-Nature and the Old Testament Theophanies\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We can now also offer a careful, honest reflection on a question we left properly unresolved earlier \'97 the 
\f0\b Messenger of the LORD
\f2\b0  passages.\
Per maxim #3, we declined to identify the Messenger with the Son as the early Fathers did, because no direct NT witness made that identification explicitly. But we can now note something honestly grounded in the text:\
- 
\f0\b The Father, as invisible Spirit, cannot be seen
\f2\b0  (John 1:18; 1 Tim 6:16; Ex 33:20).\
- 
\f0\b Yet someone is seen
\f2\b0  in Genesis 16, 22, 32; Exodus 3, 33; Judges 6, 13; Isaiah 6; Ezekiel 1; Daniel 7 \'97 figures the OT calls "the LORD," "the Angel of the LORD," "the God of Bethel," etc.\
- 
\f0\b By strict logic from John 1:18:
\f2\b0  Whoever was seen, it was not the Father directly.\
We will leave it there, per our discipline \'97 observing the pattern without forcing a single resolution. 
\f0\b What we have established is that the Father's Spirit-Nature means He cannot be seen directly
\f2\b0 , and so any "seeing of God" in Scripture must be through some mediation or accommodation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 QUESTION 3 \'97 The Relation Between the Father (Spirit) and the Son (Image, Word made flesh, Man at the right hand)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 This is the synthesis that draws together our entire study.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

