\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 John 14:9-11 \'97 The Direct Statement\
1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "Jesus said unto him, I have been so long time with you, and hast thou not known me, Philip? he that hath seen me, hath seen my Father: how then sayest thou, Show us thy Father? Believest thou not, that I am in the Father, and the Father is in me? The words that I speak unto you, I speak not of myself: but the Father that dwelleth in me, he doeth the works. Believe me, that I am in the Father, and the Father is in me: at the least, believe me for the very works' sake."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of supreme importance for our study\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u7953 \u969 \u961 \u945 \u954 \u8060 \u962  \u7952 \u956 \u8050  \u7953 \u974 \u961 \u945 \u954 \u949 \u957  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u941 \u961 \u945 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "He that has seen Me has seen the Father."
\f2\i0 \
How can this be true, given that 
\f0\b no man has seen the Father
\f2\b0  (John 1:18)?\

\f0\b The resolution lies in the Spirit-Image relation:
\f2\b0 \
- The Father is invisible Spirit.\
- The Son is the 
\f0\b Image
\f2\b0  of that invisible Father (Col 1:15) \'97 bearing the Father's character, will, words, and works visibly.\
- Seeing Jesus is 
\f1\i seeing the Father
\f2\i0  in the only way the Father can be seen by creatures \'97 through His perfect Image. Not seeing the Father's essence directly (which remains invisible), but seeing 
\f0\b what the Father is like, what He wills, what He says, what He does
\f2\b0  \'97 fully and accurately, in His Son.\
This is exactly what John 1:18 prepared us for: 
\f1\i "No man hath seen God at any time: the only begotten Son... He hath declared Him."
\f2\i0  
\f0\b The Son exegetes the invisible Father
\f2\b0  to His creatures.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u947 \u8060  \u7952 \u957  \u964 \u8183  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u8054  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u8052 \u961  \u7952 \u957  \u7952 \u956 \u959 \u943 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "I am in the Father, and the Father is in Me."
\f2\i0 \
This is the 
\f0\b mutual indwelling
\f2\b0  that prepares for the indwelling theme of Part F. The Father (as Spirit) indwells the Son; the Son is in the Father (in His Spirit-presence). This is 
\f0\b not a paradox
\f2\b0  \'97 it is the natural relation of Spirit and embodied Image.\

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u948 \u8050  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u8052 \u961  \u8001  \u7952 \u957  \u7952 \u956 \u959 \u8054  \u956 \u941 \u957 \u969 \u957  \u960 \u959 \u953 \u949 \u8150  \u964 \u8048  \u7956 \u961 \u947 \u945  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the Father that dwelleth in Me, He doeth the works."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b This is the decisive theological statement.
\f2\b0  Jesus does not say 
\f1\i "I do the works."
\f2\i0  He says 
\f1\i "the Father, dwelling in Me, does His works."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b The Father (Spirit) dwells in the Son (Image) and accomplishes the Father's works through the Son.
\f2\b0  This is the entire pattern of redemption:\
- The Father wills; the Son obeys.\
- The Father speaks; the Son utters.\
- The Father sends; the Son comes.\
- The Father acts; the Son is the medium of His action.\
- The Father is glorified; the Son is the One in whom the Father is glorified.\
Throughout Jesus' ministry, 
\f0\b the Father (Spirit) was dwelling in the Son (Man) and accomplishing the Father's works
\f2\b0 . Jesus' miracles, His teachings, His self-giving on the cross \'97 all of these were 
\f0\b the Father's works done through the Son.
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

