\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The Spirit's Anointing of the Son\
Acts 10:38\
1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "To wit, how God anointed Jesus of Nazareth with the holy Ghost, and with power: who went about doing good, and healing all that were oppressed of the devil: for God was with him."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The Father anointed the Son with the Holy Spirit and with power.
\f2\b0  Throughout His ministry, the Son operated 
\f0\b by the Spirit of the Father
\f2\b0 . This is consistent with:\
- 
\f0\b Isaiah 11:2 LXX
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the Spirit of God shall rest upon Him..."
\f2\i0 \
- 
\f0\b Isaiah 61:1 LXX
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the Spirit of the Lord is upon Me, because He has anointed Me..."
\f2\i0 \
- 
\f0\b Matthew 3:16
\f2\b0  \'97 the Spirit descending on Jesus at His baptism.\
- 
\f0\b Luke 4:1
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "Jesus, being full of the Holy Ghost, returned from Jordan."
\f2\i0 \
- 
\f0\b Luke 4:14
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "And Jesus returned in the power of the Spirit."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b The Son, as embodied Man, was filled with and operated by the Father's Spirit.
\f2\b0  This is the model and prototype of how the Father (Spirit) dwells in His Image (the Son, Man), and through Him accomplishes His works.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The Theological Coherence \'97 A Summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Here is what the whole counsel establishes about the Father-Son relation in light of the Father's Spirit-Nature:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 1. The Father, as Eternal Spirit, is invisible, omnipresent, and uncontainable.
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b 2. The Son is the Father's perfect Image \'97 the embodied expression of the invisible Father.
\f2\b0 \
- In His pre-incarnate state: the Word with God (John 1:1), the Radiance of His glory (Heb 1:3).\
- In His incarnate state: the Word made flesh (John 1:14), the Image of the invisible God in human form (Col 1:15; 2:9).\
- In His exalted state: the Man Christ Jesus at the right hand (1 Tim 2:5; Heb 10:12), the Lamb on the throne (Rev 5; 22).\

\f0\b 3. The Father (Spirit) indwells the Son (Image) \'97 "the Father dwells in Me" (John 14:10).
\f2\b0 \
- The Father's Spirit anointed the Son for His ministry (Acts 10:38; Luke 4:14).\
- The Father's works were done through the Son (John 14:10).\
- The Father's words were spoken by the Son (John 8:28; 12:49-50).\

\f0\b 4. The Son is in the Father \'97 "I in Him" (John 10:38; 14:11).
\f2\b0 \
- Drawing from the Father's life (John 5:26; 6:57).\
- Living unto the Father's glory (John 17:1, 4).\
- Subjected to the Father in eternal order (1 Cor 15:28).\

\f0\b 5. This Father-Son union \'97 Spirit-Image, indweller-indwelt \'97 is the prototype for the union the Father intends with the redeemed.
\f2\b0 \
And this brings us beautifully to your next thread, which we will take up in Part F.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b GRAND SYNTHESIS \'97 PART E COMPLETED
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 What is Decisively Established\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 By the whole counsel of Scripture \'97 OT and NT, by direct witness from Jesus Himself and from the Apostles \'97 we have established:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The Nature and Essence of the Father:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 1. 
\f0\b He is Spirit by Nature
\f2\b0  (John 4:24; Gen 1:2; Num 16:22).\
2. 
\f0\b He is invisible
\f2\b0  (John 1:18; 1 Tim 1:17; 6:16; Col 1:15).\
3. 
\f0\b He is immortal/incorruptible
\f2\b0  (1 Tim 1:17; 6:16; Rom 1:23).\
4. 
\f0\b He dwells in unapproachable light
\f2\b0  (1 Tim 6:16; Ps 104:2 LXX).\
5. 
\f0\b He is omnipresent \'97 fills heaven and earth
\f2\b0  (Jer 23:24; Ps 138:7-10; Acts 17:28).\
6. 
\f0\b He is uncontainable \'97 no place can hold Him
\f2\b0  (1 Kgs 8:27; Isa 66:1; Acts 7:48-49).\
7. 
\f0\b He is the Father of spirits
\f2\b0  (Num 16:22; Heb 12:9).\
8. 
\f0\b He is the One God
\f2\b0  (Deut 6:4; Isa 44-46; John 17:3; 1 Cor 8:6; 1 Tim 2:5).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The Implications for Our Understanding:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 1. 
\f0\b The "singular throne" of the Apocalypse is no oddity
\f2\b0  \'97 the Father (Spirit) fills the throne and surrounds it; the Lamb (embodied Man) occupies the visible center; there is no spatial conflict.\
2. 
\f0\b The Old Testament theophanies must be mediated
\f2\b0  \'97 the Father, as invisible Spirit, cannot be seen directly; any "seeing of God" is through accommodation, image, or representation.\
3. 
\f0\b The Father's omnipresence undergirds His ability to indwell
\f2\b0  \'97 He indwelt the temple, indwelt His Son, indwells His Church, and will eternally dwell with His redeemed.\
4. 
\f0\b The Father-Son union is Spirit-Image: the Eternal Spirit indwelling and operating through His perfect, embodied Image \'97 the Man Christ Jesus.
\f2\b0  This is the prototype of the indwelling we will examine in Part F.\
5. 
\f0\b The Shema is preserved unbroken
\f2\b0  \'97 the Father remains the One God; the Son is the Father's Beloved Only-Begotten through whom the Father acts; the Spirit is the Father's own essential being and active presence.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 A Note on the "Holy Spirit" Question\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 You have rightly noted at the outset of our study that the formalization of "the Holy Spirit as the third Person of the Trinity" was a post-Nicene addition. Per maxim #3, we have set that aside. But honest reading of Scripture requires us to note what 
\f1\i is
\f2\i0  there:\
- 
\f0\b The Spirit of God
\f2\b0  (Gen 1:2; Ps 51:11; Isa 11:2; etc.) \'97 the Father's own Spirit, His essential being and active presence.\
- 
\f0\b The Spirit of the LORD
\f2\b0  (Judg 3:10; 1 Sam 10:6; Isa 61:1; etc.) \'97 the same.\
- 
\f0\b The Holy Spirit / Holy Spirit of God
\f2\b0  (Ps 51:11; Eph 4:30) \'97 the Father's Spirit, marked as set apart, holy.\
- 
\f0\b The Spirit of Christ / Spirit of His Son
\f2\b0  (Rom 8:9; Gal 4:6) \'97 interestingly, the same Spirit is called both the Father's Spirit and the Son's Spirit, because the Father (Spirit) indwells the Son and pours forth that same Spirit upon the redeemed through Him.\
The honest, undogmatic, biblical reading is this: 
\f0\b the Holy Spirit is the Father's own Spirit
\f2\b0  \'97 His essential being and active presence \'97 which is poured out through the exalted Son upon the redeemed (Acts 2:33), so that the Father may indwell His people 
\f0\b in Christ
\f2\b0  by His Spirit.\
This will become explicit and beautifully clear in Part F.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 READY FOR PART F\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We are now properly prepared for 
\f0\b Part F \'97 The Indwelling: That We Should Be the Habitation of God in Christ.
\f2\b0 \
Having established that 
\f0\b the Father is Eternal Spirit who indwelt His Son
\f2\b0 , we can now see the breathtaking design: 
\f0\b the Father purposes that we, the elect, should likewise become the habitation of God in Christ
\f2\b0  \'97 drawn into the very Father-Son union that has existed from eternity.\
Shall we proceed to 
\f0\b Part F
\f2\b0 , beginning with 
\f0\b John 17:20-26 (the High Priestly Prayer)
\f2\b0  and 
\f0\b John 14:16-23 (the promise of mutual indwelling)
\f2\b0  as the foundation?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 How does the Father's Spirit-Nature inform our understanding of the 'seven Spirits before the throne' in Revelation \'97 can we say anything honest about them without overreaching?\
exactly the kind of careful probe our maxims require. Let us proceed with strict discipline: examining every text where the phrase occurs, cross-referencing all relevant OT and NT witnesses, and being honest about what we can and cannot establish.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
