\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 WHAT WE CAN HONESTLY SAY (PER OUR MAXIMS)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Now let us be careful and honest about what is established and what is not.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Established by Cross-Witness (Two or More Honest Parallels):\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 1. The "seven Spirits" are directly tied to the imagery of Zechariah 4
\f2\b0  \'97 the seven lamps and the seven eyes of the LORD that range through all the earth.\

\f0\b 2. The "seven Spirits" are directly tied to the Spirit of the LORD on the Messiah in Isaiah 11:2 LXX
\f2\b0  \'97 which lists seven aspects/operations of the one Spirit of the Father.\

\f0\b 3. The "seven Spirits" belong to the Father
\f2\b0  (Rev 3:1; 4:5; 5:6 \'97 
\f1\i "of God"
\f2\i0 ) \'97 they are His.\

\f0\b 4. The "seven Spirits" are now held by the Son
\f2\b0  (Rev 3:1) and represented as His 
\f0\b eyes
\f2\b0  (Rev 5:6) \'97 meaning the Father's omniscient Spirit-vision is now exercised through the exalted Son.\

\f0\b 5. The number "seven" in Apocalyptic usage is the number of completeness / fullness
\f2\b0  \'97 seven churches (representing the whole Church), seven seals (representing the complete unfolding of judgment), seven trumpets (the complete proclamation), seven bowls (the complete outpouring of wrath), etc. Per this consistent symbolic usage, 
\f0\b "seven Spirits" most naturally signifies the 
\f3\i fullness
\f0\i0  or 
\f3\i completeness
\f0\i0  of God's Spirit
\f2\b0  \'97 His Spirit in its perfect, complete operation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The Most Honest Reading That Honors the Whole Counsel\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The "seven Spirits before the throne" are the Father's own Spirit in its sevenfold fullness \'97 the complete, perfect operation of the One Spirit of God \'97 now held and sent forth by the Lamb into all the earth.
\f2\b0 \
This reading:\
- Honors the Shema (one God, the Father \'97 Deut 6:4) \'97 the Spirit is 
\f1\i His Spirit
\f2\i0 , not seven separate Spirits.\
- Honors Isaiah 11:2 LXX \'97 seven aspects of the one Spirit of the LORD.\
- Honors Zechariah 4:6, 10 \'97 the LORD's Spirit and the LORD's seven eyes through the earth.\
- Honors the consistent Apocalyptic symbolism of "seven" as fullness/completeness.\
- Honors what we established in Part E \'97 the Father's Spirit is His omnipresent essential being.\
- Honors the Son's role \'97 the Lamb has the Spirit and pours it out (Acts 2:33; John 7:39; Rev 5:6).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 What We Should NOT Say (Overreaching)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 1. We should not say
\f2\b0  the seven Spirits are 
\f0\b seven distinct persons
\f2\b0  of a sevenfold Spirit-Godhead. There is no Scriptural warrant for this. The text gives us 
\f1\i seven
\f2\i0  as a symbol of fullness, not as a literal count of distinct personal entities.\

\f0\b 2. We should not say
\f2\b0  the seven Spirits are 
\f0\b the "Holy Spirit" of post-Nicene Trinitarian theology
\f2\b0  \'97 that is, the supposed "Third Person of the Trinity, co-equal with the Father and the Son." Per maxim #3, we have set aside that formulation. Scripture itself uses 
\f1\i "Spirit of God,"
\f2\i0  
\f1\i "Spirit of the LORD,"
\f2\i0  
\f1\i "Holy Spirit,"
\f2\i0  etc., to refer to the Father's own essential being and active presence \'97 not a separately-personal Deity.\

\f0\b 3. We should not say
\f2\b0  the seven Spirits are 
\f0\b seven angels
\f2\b0  or 
\f0\b the seven archangels
\f2\b0  of some intertestamental traditions. While some have proposed this (because the text says they are "sent into all the earth"), the cross-references to Isaiah 11 and Zechariah 4 establish them as 
\f0\b the Father's Spirit in His sevenfold operation
\f2\b0 , not as created angelic beings.\

\f0\b 4. We should not press the imagery beyond what it warrants.
\f2\b0  The text uses 
\f1\i symbolic visual language
\f2\i0 : seven lamps, seven eyes, seven horns. These are not to be flattened into mechanical literal descriptions. They are John's visionary representations of spiritual realities.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 THE THEOLOGICAL COHERENCE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 When we put this together with everything we have established in Part E, here is what we have:\
1. 
\f0\b The Father is Eternal Spirit
\f2\b0  (John 4:24) \'97 invisible, omnipresent, uncontainable.\
2. 
\f0\b The Father's Spirit is His own essential being and active presence
\f2\b0  throughout creation \'97 His omnipresent, all-seeing, all-knowing Presence.\
3. 
\f0\b The "seven Spirits of God"
\f2\b0  in the Apocalypse are the symbolic representation of this Spirit in His 
\f0\b complete fullness
\f2\b0  (per Isa 11:2 LXX) and 
\f0\b omnipresent operation through all the earth
\f2\b0  (per Zech 4:10).\
4. 
\f0\b The seven Spirits are "before the throne"
\f2\b0  \'97 i.e., they are the Father's own Spirit in His full operation, proceeding from His presence.\
5. 
\f0\b The Son has the seven Spirits
\f2\b0  (Rev 3:1) and they are His 
\f0\b eyes
\f2\b0  (Rev 5:6) \'97 meaning the exalted Son now holds and exercises the Father's omniscient Spirit-vision, having received the Father's Spirit at His exaltation (Acts 2:33) and now pouring it forth.\
6. 
\f0\b They are "sent into all the earth"
\f2\b0  \'97 the Father's Spirit, through the Son, goes forth into all the creation to accomplish His purposes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 The Beautiful Picture\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 What emerges, when we read with the lens you have given us, is breathtaking:\
- 
\f0\b The Father (Spirit)
\f2\b0  fills heaven and earth, but His throne is in heaven.\
- 
\f0\b The Lamb (the Man Christ Jesus)
\f2\b0  sits at the right hand of the throne in glorified humanity.\
- 
\f0\b The Father's Spirit, in His sevenfold fullness
\f2\b0 , proceeds from the throne \'97 symbolized as 
\f0\b seven lamps of fire
\f2\b0  before the throne (the Father's Spirit-presence radiating outward) and as 
\f0\b the seven eyes of the Lamb
\f2\b0  (the Father's Spirit now operating through the exalted Son).\
- 
\f0\b The Spirit is sent into all the earth
\f2\b0  \'97 to convict, to seal, to indwell the redeemed, to accomplish the Father's saving purposes through the Son.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 This is one God, the Father, acting through His Beloved Only-Begotten Son, by His own Spirit
\f2\b0  \'97 the consistent pattern from Genesis to Revelation, now fully revealed at the climax of all prophecy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 A FINAL HONEST NOTE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We have done what our maxims allow:\
- We have noted what the text 
\f0\b plainly says.
\f2\b0 \
- We have cross-referenced 
\f0\b OT and NT witnesses with clear parallels
\f2\b0  (Isaiah 11, Zechariah 4, 2 Chron 16:9, Ps 138).\
- We have identified 
\f0\b the most natural reading
\f2\b0  that honors the whole counsel.\
- We have refused to 
\f0\b flatten the symbolism into either literal seven-person-spirits or post-Nicene Trinitarian categories.
\f2\b0 \
- We have acknowledged that 
\f0\b even the most natural reading remains a reading
\f2\b0  \'97 the text uses symbolic apocalyptic imagery, and we should hold our interpretation with appropriate humility.\
What we 
\f0\b can
\f2\b0  firmly say is this: 
\f0\b The seven Spirits are most naturally understood as the Father's own Spirit in His sevenfold fullness \'97 present before the throne, held and exercised by the exalted Lamb, sent into all the earth to accomplish the Father's purposes.
\f2\b0  This is consistent with the Shema, with Isaiah 11:2, with Zechariah 4:6, 10, with the Father's Spirit-Nature established in Part E, and with the consistent pattern of the Father acting through His Son by His Spirit.\
What we 
\f0\b cannot
\f2\b0  assert with full witness-establishment is the precise metaphysical nature of these seven Spirits beyond this \'97 and we ought not to try.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 READY TO RETURN TO PART F\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We have now properly addressed the "seven Spirits" question without overreach. Our Part E foundation is complete:\
- The Father is Eternal Spirit (established).\
- The seven Spirits are the Father's Spirit in sevenfold fullness (established as the most natural reading).\
- The Father (Spirit) indwells the Son and operates through Him (established).\
- The Spirit is sent into all the earth through the exalted Lamb (established).\
This now perfectly sets us up for 
\f0\b Part F \'97 The Indwelling: That We Should Be the Habitation of God in Christ.
\f2\b0  The Father's Spirit, having dwelt fully in the Son, is now poured out through the Son upon the elect, so that 
\f0\b we may become the habitation of the Father in His Son by His Spirit
\f2\b0  \'97 drawn into the very Father-Son union that has existed from eternity.\
Shall we now proceed to 
\f0\b Part F
\f2\b0 ?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 Before we move to Part F, can you address how Acts 2:33 fits with this picture of the Son receiving and pouring out the Father's Spirit?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Acts 2:33 is 
\f0\b one of the most theologically precise statements in the entire NT
\f2\b0  about the relationship between the Father, the Son, and the Spirit at the Son's exaltation. It deserves the most careful examination per our maxims.\
Let me proceed with the same discipline as before \'97 examining the verse in context, parsing the Greek carefully, cross-referencing all relevant witnesses, and being honest about what is established and what remains to be carefully held.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b ACTS 2:33 \'97 THE EXALTED SON RECEIVES AND POURS FORTH THE PROMISE OF THE SPIRIT
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 The Verse in Context \'97 Acts 2:32-36\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Let me first set Acts 2:33 in its immediate context, because it is the climax of Peter's Pentecost sermon and connects directly to Psalm 110:1.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 This Jesus hath God raised up, whereof we all are witnesses. Since then that he by the right hand of God hath been exalted, and hath received of his Father the promise of the holy Ghost, he hath shed forth this which ye now see and hear. For David is not ascended into heaven, but he saith, The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand, Until I make thine enemies thy footestoole 
\f1\i (footstool)
\f2\i0 . Therefore, let all the house of Israel know for a surety, that God hath made him both Lord, and Christ, this Jesus, I say, whom ye have crucified. \'97 
\f0\b Acts 2:32-36, 1599 Geneva
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 CLAUSE-BY-CLAUSE EXAMINATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Let me walk through verse 33 phrase by phrase.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Clause 1: \uc0\u964 \u8135  \u948 \u949 \u958 \u953 \u8119  \u959 \u8022 \u957  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166  \u8017 \u968 \u969 \u952 \u949 \u8054 \u962 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "Therefore, having been exalted by/to the right hand of God..."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Critical grammatical note:
\f2\b0  The phrase 
\f0\b \uc0\u964 \u8135  \u948 \u949 \u958 \u953 \u8119  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 
\f2\b0  is in the 
\f0\b dative case
\f2\b0 . The dative can be read in two ways here:\
- 
\f0\b Instrumental dative:
\f2\b0  
\f1\i "by the right hand of God"
\f2\i0  \'97 i.e., 
\f0\b the Father's right hand (His power/agency) raised the Son up.
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b Dative of location:
\f2\b0  
\f1\i "to the right hand of God"
\f2\i0  \'97 i.e., 
\f0\b the Son was raised up to the place at the Father's right hand.
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b Both readings are grammatically valid.
\f2\b0  The Geneva translates: 
\f1\i "by the right hand of God hath been exalted."
\f2\i0  This honestly captures the primary sense \'97 
\f0\b the Father's agency exalted the Son
\f2\b0  \'97 while also implying the location (the place to which He was exalted).\

\f0\b What is decisively established by both readings:
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b The Son did not exalt Himself.
\f2\b0  The verb 
\f0\b \uc0\u8017 \u968 \u969 \u952 \u949 \u943 \u962 
\f2\b0  is a passive participle \'97 
\f1\i "having been exalted."
\f2\i0  He is the recipient of the action.\
- 
\f0\b The Father is the agent
\f2\b0  \'97 the One who exalted Him.\
- 
\f0\b The Son's present location is at the Father's right hand
\f2\b0  \'97 consistent with Psalm 110:1, which Peter explicitly cites in v. 34.\
This perfectly preserves our foundation: 
\f0\b the Father is the One who acts; the Son is the One who receives.
\f2\b0  Even at His exaltation, the Son does not lift Himself \'97 the Father lifts Him.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Clause 2: \uc0\u964 \u942 \u957  \u964 \u949  \u7952 \u960 \u945 \u947 \u947 \u949 \u955 \u943 \u945 \u957  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u928 \u957 \u949 \u973 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u959 \u962  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7945 \u947 \u943 \u959 \u965  \u955 \u945 \u946 \u8060 \u957  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u8048  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u8056 \u962 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "having received the promise of the Holy Spirit from the Father..."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 This is the theologically decisive clause.
\f2\b0  Let me examine each element.\

\f0\b (1) \uc0\u964 \u8052 \u957  \u7952 \u960 \u945 \u947 \u947 \u949 \u955 \u943 \u945 \u957  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u928 \u957 \u949 \u973 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u959 \u962  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7945 \u947 \u943 \u959 \u965 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the promise of the Holy Spirit."
\f2\i0 \
The genitive 
\f0\b \uc0\u964 \u959 \u8166  \u928 \u957 \u949 \u973 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  is a 
\f0\b genitive of content / apposition
\f2\b0  \'97 meaning 
\f0\b the promise consists of / is the Holy Spirit.
\f2\b0  This is consistent with:\
- 
\f0\b Luke 24:49
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "behold, I send the promise of My Father upon you."
\f2\i0 \
- 
\f0\b Acts 1:4
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "wait for the promise of the Father."
\f2\i0 \
- 
\f0\b Galatians 3:14
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "that we might receive the promise of the Spirit through faith."
\f2\i0 \
- 
\f0\b Ephesians 1:13
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "sealed with the Holy Spirit of promise."
\f2\i0 \
The promise = the Spirit. The Spirit promised = the Father's own Spirit promised through the OT prophets (Joel 2:28-32 LXX \'97 which Peter has just cited in Acts 2:17-21).\

\f0\b (2) \uc0\u955 \u945 \u946 \u8060 \u957  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u8048  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u972 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "having received from beside the Father."
\f2\i0 \
The verb 
\f0\b \uc0\u955 \u945 \u946 \u8060 \u957 
\f2\b0  is an aorist participle of 
\f0\b \uc0\u955 \u945 \u956 \u946 \u940 \u957 \u969 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "to receive, to take."
\f2\i0  The Son 
\f0\b received
\f2\b0 . He is the recipient.\
The preposition 
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u945 \u961 \u940 
\f2\b0  + genitive (
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u945 \u961 \u8048  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u8056 \u962 
\f2\b0 ) \'97 
\f1\i "from beside / from the presence of / from"
\f2\i0  \'97 denotes 
\f0\b the Father as the Source
\f2\b0 . The Spirit is 
\f0\b from the Father
\f2\b0 , given to the Son.\

\f0\b This is the same construction Jesus Himself used:
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 But when that Comforter shall come, whom I will send unto you from the Father, even the Spirit of truth, which proceedeth of the Father, he shall testify of me. \'97 
\f0\b John 15:26, 1599 Geneva
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Two parallel phrases
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "whom I will send from the Father"
\f2\i0  and 
\f1\i "which proceedeth of the Father"
\f2\i0  \'97 both use 
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u945 \u961 \u8048  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u972 \u962 
\f2\b0 . 
\f0\b The Spirit proceeds from the Father
\f2\b0  as His own essential being and presence.\

\f0\b (3) The theological coherence:
\f2\b0 \
Putting this together: 
\f0\b The Spirit belongs to the Father as His own.
\f2\b0  The exalted Son 
\f0\b receives
\f2\b0  this promised Spirit 
\f0\b from
\f2\b0  the Father. The Son does not have the Spirit as an independent possession \'97 He 
\f0\b receives
\f2\b0  it from the Father at His exaltation, 
\f1\i as the One who is now seated at the Father's right hand and qualified to dispense it to His people.
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Clause 3: \uc0\u7952 \u958 \u941 \u967 \u949 \u949 \u957  \u964 \u959 \u8166 \u964 \u959  \u8003  \u8017 \u956 \u949 \u8150 \u962  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u946 \u955 \u941 \u960 \u949 \u964 \u949  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u7936 \u954 \u959 \u973 \u949 \u964 \u949 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "He has poured forth this which you both see and hear."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 (1) \uc0\u7952 \u958 \u941 \u967 \u949 \u949 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 aorist active of 
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u954 \u967 \u941 \u969 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "to pour out, to pour forth."
\f2\i0  This is the verb of 
\f0\b outpouring
\f2\b0  \'97 used in the LXX of Joel 2:28-29 (which Peter has just cited):\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 And it shall be after these things, I will pour out of my spirit on all flesh \'85 Even on the male and female slaves, in those days, I will pour out of my spirit. \'97 
\f0\b Joel 2:28-29 LXX, NETS
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Observation of supreme importance:
\f2\b0  In Joel, 
\f0\b it is the LORD (the Father) who says "I will pour out My Spirit."
\f2\b0  The Father is the Pourer; the Spirit is 
\f0\b His own Spirit
\f2\b0 .\

\f0\b In Acts 2:33, Peter says: the exalted Son has poured forth this Spirit.
\f2\b0 \
How are these two statements reconciled? Beautifully, by the pattern we have established throughout:\
- 
\f0\b The Father is the Source.
\f2\b0  The Spirit is 
\f0\b His
\f2\b0  Spirit. He promised it through Joel.\
- 
\f0\b The Son is the Agent.
\f2\b0  Having been exalted to the Father's right hand and having received the Spirit from the Father, 
\f0\b the Son is now the One through whom the Father pours out His own Spirit upon all flesh.
\f2\b0 \
This is precisely the pattern of 1 Corinthians 8:6: 
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u958  \u959 \u8023  \u964 \u8048  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945 
\f2\b0  (the Father, from whom) and 
\f0\b \uc0\u948 \u953 \u8125  \u959 \u8023  \u964 \u8048  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945 
\f2\b0  (the Son, through whom). 
\f0\b The Spirit comes from the Father, through the Son, to the people.
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b (2) \uc0\u964 \u959 \u8166 \u964 \u959  \u8003  \u8017 \u956 \u949 \u8150 \u962  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u946 \u955 \u941 \u960 \u949 \u964 \u949  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u7936 \u954 \u959 \u973 \u949 \u964 \u949 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "this which you both see and hear."
\f2\i0  The visible and audible phenomena of Pentecost \'97 the rushing wind, the tongues of fire, the speaking in languages \'97 are the manifest evidence of the Spirit's outpouring.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 WHAT ACTS 2:33 ESTABLISHES \'97 DECISIVELY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Let me state plainly what is established by this verse, in conjunction with the cross-witnesses:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1. The Father is the Source of the Spirit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - The Spirit is 
\f0\b promised by the Father
\f2\b0  (Luke 24:49; Acts 1:4).\
- The Spirit 
\f0\b proceeds from the Father
\f2\b0  (John 15:26).\
- The Spirit is given 
\f0\b from beside the Father
\f2\b0  (Acts 2:33; John 15:26).\
- The Father said 
\f1\i "I will pour out My Spirit"
\f2\i0  in the OT promise (Joel 2:28-29 LXX).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 2. The exalted Son is the Recipient and Dispenser.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - The Son 
\f0\b received
\f2\b0  the Spirit from the Father at His exaltation (Acts 2:33).\
- The Son 
\f0\b pours forth
\f2\b0  the Spirit upon His people (Acts 2:33).\
- The Son 
\f0\b sends
\f2\b0  the Spirit from the Father (John 15:26).\
- This is the consistent pattern of 
\f1\i from the Father, through the Son.
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 3. The Spirit is the Father's own essential being and active presence \'97 now operating through the exalted Son.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 This is consistent with everything we established in Part E:\
- The Father is Eternal Spirit (John 4:24).\
- His Spirit fills heaven and earth (Jer 23:24).\
- His Spirit is His omnipresent Presence (Ps 138:7-10).\
- Now, post-Pentecost, this Spirit is 
\f0\b poured out through the Son upon all flesh
\f2\b0  \'97 extending the Father's indwelling Presence to the redeemed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 4. This perfectly preserves the Shema and our foundation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - The Father remains the 
\f0\b One God
\f2\b0 , the sole Source.\
- The Son remains the 
\f0\b Beloved Son
\f2\b0 , exalted by the Father, receiving from the Father, acting for the Father.\
- The Spirit is 
\f0\b the Father's own Spirit
\f2\b0  \'97 not a separate Deity in the post-Nicene sense, but the Father's essential Spirit-Nature now being extended through the Son to His people.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 A CRITICAL POINT \'97 WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE "SEVEN SPIRITS" AND PART F\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Acts 2:33 confirms exactly what we noted in the "seven Spirits" examination:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 The exalted Son has received the Father's Spirit and now exercises it / dispenses it.
\f2\b0 \
- In Rev 3:1, the Son 
\f0\b has
\f2\b0  the seven Spirits of God.\
- In Rev 5:6, the seven Spirits are the Son's 
\f0\b eyes
\f2\b0 , 
\f0\b sent into all the earth
\f2\b0 .\
- In Acts 2:33, the Son has 
\f0\b received
\f2\b0  the Spirit from the Father and 
\f0\b pours it forth
\f2\b0 .\

\f0\b Same theology, same pattern:
\f2\b0  The Father's Spirit, in its fullness, is now held and exercised by the exalted Son, and from Him is poured out into all the earth upon the elect.\
This is also what prepares us for 
\f0\b Part F \'97 the Indwelling.
\f2\b0  Because:\
- 
\f0\b The Father (Spirit) indwelt the Son
\f2\b0  during His earthly ministry (John 14:10; Acts 10:38).\
- 
\f0\b At His exaltation, the Son received the Spirit from the Father
\f2\b0  in fullness (Acts 2:33).\
- 
\f0\b The Son now pours out the Spirit upon His elect
\f2\b0  \'97 so that the Father, by His Spirit, indwells the redeemed 
\f0\b in the Son
\f2\b0 .\
- 
\f0\b The Father-Son union now becomes the model for our union with God
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "that they all may be one, as Thou Father art in Me, and I in Thee, that they also may be one in Us"
\f2\i0  (John 17:21).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 ANOTHER CROSS-WITNESS WORTH NOTING \'97 John 7:37-39\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 \'85If any man thirst, let him come unto me, and drink. He that believeth in me, as saith the Scripture, out of his belly shall flow rivers of water of life. (This spake he of the Spirit which they that believed in him, should receive: for the holy Ghost was not yet given, because that Jesus was not yet glorified.) \'97 
\f0\b John 7:37-39, 1599 Geneva
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 John 7:39 is the perfect predictor of what happens at Acts 2:33:\
- 
\f0\b "The Holy Spirit was not yet given"
\f2\b0  \'97 \uc0\u959 \u8020 \u960 \u969  \u947 \u8048 \u961  \u7974 \u957  \u928 \u957 \u949 \u8166 \u956 \u945  \'97 because Jesus was not yet glorified.\
- 
\f0\b The Spirit's outpouring waited on the Son's glorification.
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b At Pentecost (Acts 2:33), Jesus, now glorified, receives the Spirit from the Father and pours it forth.
\f2\b0 \
The two passages perfectly cohere. The Spirit waited; the Son was exalted; the Spirit was poured.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

