\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "...Holy Father, keep them in thy Name, even them whom thou hast given me, that they may be one, as we are."
\f2\i0 \
This is the first introduction of 
\f0\b "one as We are"
\f2\b0  language. The Father's Name has been 
\f0\b given
\f2\b0  (\uc0\u948 \u941 \u948 \u969 \u954 \u940 \u962 ) to the Son \'97 and the disciples are to be kept in that Name. The oneness of the Father and the Son is the 
\f0\b prototype
\f2\b0  for the oneness Jesus desires for His people.\
We must hold this verse carefully \'97 Jesus does 
\f0\b not
\f2\b0  ask that the disciples be "one 
\f1\i with
\f2\i0  Us" here (that comes in v. 21). He asks that they be "one 
\f0\b as
\f2\b0  We are." The model is given; the application will be unfolded shortly.\
