\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 CATEGORY 3 \'97 SUFFERING IN THE FLESH AS DYING WITH CHRIST DAILY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 A more interior, ongoing form: the daily mortification of the flesh and the bearing about of Christ's death in the body.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 2 Corinthians 4:8-11:\
1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "We are afflicted on every side, yet are we not in distress: we are in doubt, but yet we despair not. We are persecuted, but not forsaken: cast down, but we perish not. Everywhere we bear about in our body the dying of the Lord Jesus, that the life of Jesus might also be made manifest in our bodies. For we which live, are always delivered unto death for Jesus' sake, that the life also of Jesus might be made manifest in our mortal flesh."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of profound significance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  The apostolic ministry is described in 
\f0\b four pairs of paradoxes:
\f2\b0  afflicted but not crushed, perplexed but not despairing, persecuted but not forsaken, struck down but not destroyed. The believer experiences the reality of pressure, perplexity, persecution, and being knocked down \'97 yet is sustained.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u959 \u964 \u949  \u964 \u8052 \u957  \u957 \u941 \u954 \u961 \u969 \u963 \u953 \u957  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7992 \u951 \u963 \u959 \u8166  \u7952 \u957  \u964 \u8183  \u963 \u974 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u953  \u960 \u949 \u961 \u953 \u966 \u941 \u961 \u959 \u957 \u964 \u949 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "always bearing about in the body the dying of Jesus."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b \uc0\u957 \u941 \u954 \u961 \u969 \u963 \u953 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the dying, the state of being put to death."
\f2\i0  The believer 
\f0\b always carries about
\f2\b0  the dying of Jesus in his body. This is more than physical suffering; it is a continual experiential identification with the Son's crucifixion.\

\f0\b (3) The purpose:
\f2\b0  
\f1\i "that the life of Jesus might also be manifested in our body."
\f2\i0  The carrying-about of Christ's dying is unto the manifestation of Christ's life. 
\f0\b Death precedes life \'97 even in the believer's daily experience.
\f2\b0  The pattern of the Son becomes the pattern of His people.\

\f0\b (4)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7936 \u949 \u8054  \u947 \u8048 \u961  \u7969 \u956 \u949 \u8150 \u962  \u959 \u7985  \u950 \u8182 \u957 \u964 \u949 \u962  \u949 \u7984 \u962  \u952 \u940 \u957 \u945 \u964 \u959 \u957  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u948 \u953 \u948 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u952 \u945  \u948 \u953 \u8048  \u7992 \u951 \u963 \u959 \u8166 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "always we who live are delivered unto death for Jesus' sake."
\f2\i0  The verb 
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u948 \u953 \u948 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u952 \u945 
\f2\b0  is the same verb used of Jesus being delivered up for our sins (Rom 4:25; 8:32). Paul applies it to the believer: 
\f0\b we are continually being delivered up to death for His sake.
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Romans 8:36 \'97 A citation of Psalm 43:23 LXX (Ps 44:22):\
1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "As it is written, For thy sake are we killed all day long: we are counted as sheep for the slaughter."
