\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b A FINAL OBSERVATION \'97 THE THEOLOGICAL HEART OF IT
\f2\b0 \
Romans 8:17 must be read in the context of Romans 8:18-39 \'97 the most exalted passage on the believer's security in Christ. Paul has just said the conditional 
\f1\i "if we suffer with Him."
\f2\i0  Within the next 22 verses he declares:\
- The sufferings are not worthy to be compared with the coming glory.\
- All things work together for good.\
- Those whom the Father foreknew, He predestined, called, justified, and glorified.\
- 
\f0\b If God is for us, who can be against us?
\f2\b0 \
- He who did not spare His own Son will freely give us all things.\
- 
\f0\b No one can lay any charge against God's elect.
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b Christ Himself intercedes for us at the Father's right hand.
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b Nothing \'97 not tribulation, distress, persecution, famine, nakedness, peril, sword, death, life, angels, principalities, powers, things present, things to come, height, depth, nor any other creature \'97 can separate us from the love of God in Christ Jesus our Lord.
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 This is the framework in which the call to co-suffering is given.
\f2\b0  The Father is not asking us to bear sufferings He has not foreseen, not allowed, not measured, not woven into His glorious purpose. 
\f0\b Every suffering we endure with Christ is endured in His unfailing love and unbreakable purpose.
\f2\b0 \
The Beloved Son suffered for the joy set before Him (Heb 12:2). We suffer with Him for the joy set before us \'97 the coming glory, the unveiled face of the Father, the eternal abode in His house, the inheritance reserved in heaven that cannot fade away.\

\f0\b Christ has made our sufferings holy.
\f2\b0  What He endured, He sanctified. What we endure in fellowship with Him is now caught up into His own purpose. We are not merely sufferers; we are 
\f0\b co-sufferers with the Beloved
\f2\b0 , 
\f0\b for the eternal weight of glory
\f2\b0  that no eye has seen.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b A QUESTION FOR YOU
\f2\b0 \
Brother, this is a sobering subject \'97 but a glorious one. Where would you have us go from here?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 (A)
\f2\b0  Shall we now turn to 
\f0\b the practical implications of identification with Christ
\f2\b0  \'97 how the indicative of who we are produces the imperative of how we walk (Romans 6, 8, 12; Galatians 5; Colossians 3)?\

\f0\b (B)
\f2\b0  Shall we examine 
\f0\b the corporate dimension
\f2\b0  \'97 the Body of Christ, the Bride, the Temple, the Holy Nation?\

\f0\b (C)
\f2\b0  Shall we proceed to 
\f0\b the eschatological consummation
\f2\b0  \'97 the resurrection, the marriage of the Lamb, the new heavens and new earth?\

\f0\b (D)
\f2\b0  Is there another thread on suffering with Christ you wish to pursue further?\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 Let us now proceed to (A) \'97 the practical implications of identification with Christ, beginning with how the indicative of who we are in Christ produces the imperative of how we walk in Romans 6.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We now enter what is perhaps the most practically transformative section of Pauline theology \'97 the 
\f0\b indicative-to-imperative pattern
\f2\b0  that governs all New Testament ethics. Romans 6 is the foundational chapter where this pattern is most clearly displayed.\
Per our refined modus operandi, we will:\
1. Examine the 
\f0\b logic
\f2\b0  Paul establishes \'97 how the indicative (who we 
\f1\i are
\f2\i0  in Christ) gives rise to the imperative (how we 
\f1\i walk
\f2\i0 ).\
2. Walk carefully through Romans 6 \'97 its movement of thought.\
3. Cross-reference all relevant NT witnesses that establish the same pattern.\
4. Draw out the practical implications for the modern believer.\
Let us proceed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b THE INDICATIVE-TO-IMPERATIVE PATTERN
\f2\b0 \
Before we enter Romans 6, we must understand what Paul is doing.\
Throughout the NT \'97 but especially in Paul \'97 there is a consistent structure:\
- 
\f0\b The Indicative:
\f2\b0  A declaration of what 
\f1\i is true
\f2\i0  of the believer in Christ. "You 
\f1\i are
\f2\i0  dead to sin." "You 
\f1\i have been
\f2\i0  crucified with Christ." "You 
\f1\i are
\f2\i0  in Christ Jesus." "You 
\f1\i have
\f2\i0  been raised with Him."\
- 
\f0\b The Imperative:
\f2\b0  A command based on the indicative. "Therefore, do not let sin reign." "Walk worthy." "Put off the old man." "Put on the new."\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 The order is critical.
\f2\b0  Paul never commands without first establishing the indicative. He does not say 
\f1\i "Try to become dead to sin"
\f2\i0  \'97 he says 
\f1\i "Reckon yourselves to be dead to sin, because you ARE dead to sin."
\f2\i0  The command flows from the reality, not toward it.\
This is the 
\f0\b heart of the Gospel's ethical power.
\f2\b0  The Christian life is not 
\f1\i "work hard to become what you wish you were."
\f2\i0  It is 
\f1\i "walk in the reality of what you already are in Christ."
\f2\i0  Sanctification is not the achievement of an unreal status; it is the working out, in daily life, of what is already true positionally.\
Romans 6 is the chapter where Paul most explicitly lays this pattern bare.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b THE CONTEXT \'97 Romans 5-6
\f2\b0 \
Paul has just declared in Romans 5:20-21 that 
\f0\b where sin abounded, grace did much more abound.
\f2\b0  He has just declared that grace reigns through righteousness unto eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.\
This sets up the question that opens Romans 6 \'97 the question of every shallow critic of grace: 
\f1\i "If grace abounds where sin abounds, shall we not sin more, that grace may abound more?"
\f2\i0 \
Paul's answer to this question is the entire chapter \'97 and his answer is grounded entirely in 
\f0\b the believer's identification with Christ.
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b ROMANS 6:1-2 \'97 THE QUESTION AND THE PIVOT
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "What shall we say then? Shall we continue still in sin, that grace may abound? God forbid. how shall we, that are dead to sin, live yet therein?"
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of foundational importance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  Paul's response to the question 
\f1\i "shall we continue in sin?"
\f2\i0  is 
\f0\b \uc0\u956 \u8052  \u947 \u941 \u957 \u959 \u953 \u964 \u959 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "God forbid"
\f2\i0  / 
\f1\i "may it never be"
\f2\i0  \'97 the strongest negation in Greek. The very thought is unthinkable.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  But notice carefully 
\f0\b why
\f2\b0  Paul says it is unthinkable. He does not say 
\f1\i "Because sin is bad."
\f2\i0  He does not say 
\f1\i "Because God's law forbids it."
\f2\i0  He says: 
\f0\b \uc0\u959 \u7989 \u964 \u953 \u957 \u949 \u962  \u7936 \u960 \u949 \u952 \u940 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u964 \u8135  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u8115 , \u960 \u8182 \u962  \u7956 \u964 \u953  \u950 \u942 \u963 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u7952 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8135 ;
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "We who died to sin, how shall we still live in it?"
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b This is the indicative.
\f2\b0  Before Paul will give a single command in this chapter, he establishes the 
\f0\b fact
\f2\b0  of what has happened to the believer: 
\f0\b we died to sin.
\f2\b0  The aorist tense 
\f0\b \uc0\u7936 \u960 \u949 \u952 \u940 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957 
\f2\b0  points to a definite past event \'97 
\f0\b the believer's death to sin at the cross of Christ.
\f2\b0 \
The argument is 
\f0\b ontological, not merely behavioral.
\f2\b0  Paul does not argue that the believer 
\f1\i should not
\f2\i0  continue in sin because it would be wrong. He argues that the believer 
\f1\i cannot consistently
\f2\i0  continue in sin because 
\f0\b he has died to it.
\f2\b0  Continuing in sin is 
\f0\b a contradiction of the new reality of his being.
\f2\b0 \
This is the indicative-to-imperative pattern in its purest form: the moral question is answered by a declaration of fact.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b ROMANS 6:3-5 \'97 THE FOUNDATIONAL INDICATIVE: BAPTIZED INTO HIS DEATH
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "Know ye not, that all we which have been baptized into Jesus Christ, have been baptized into his death? We are buried then with him by baptism into his death, that like as Christ was raised up from the dead to the glory of the Father, so we also should walk in newness of life. For if we be planted with him to the similitude of his death, even so shall we be to the similitude of his resurrection."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of foundational importance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7970  \u7936 \u947 \u957 \u959 \u949 \u8150 \u964 \u949 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "or are you ignorant?"
\f2\i0  Paul appeals to knowledge they should already have. The indicative is something the believer 
\f0\b must know
\f2\b0 . Ignorance of one's identification with Christ leaves the believer powerless to walk in the imperative.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8005 \u963 \u959 \u953  \u7952 \u946 \u945 \u960 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u952 \u951 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u949 \u7984 \u962  \u935 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u7992 \u951 \u963 \u959 \u8166 \u957 , \u949 \u7984 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u952 \u940 \u957 \u945 \u964 \u959 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7952 \u946 \u945 \u960 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u952 \u951 \u956 \u949 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "as many as were baptized into Christ Jesus, were baptized into His death."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b Baptism here is more than an outward rite
\f2\b0  \'97 it is the visible enactment of the believer's identification with Christ. To be baptized 
\f0\b into Christ
\f2\b0  is to be baptized 
\f0\b into His death.
\f2\b0  The two cannot be separated. We are not joined to a sin-bearing Christ at one point and detached from His death-bearing reality at another. 
\f0\b To be in Him is to be in His death.
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u963 \u965 \u957 \u949 \u964 \u940 \u966 \u951 \u956 \u949 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "we were buried together with Him."
\f2\i0  The aorist passive \'97 completed past action done to us. 
\f0\b We have been buried together with Christ.
\f2\b0  Burial is the seal of death \'97 what is buried is settled as dead.\

\f0\b (4)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b The purpose: \uc0\u7989 \u957 \u945 ... \u7952 \u957  \u954 \u945 \u953 \u957 \u972 \u964 \u951 \u964 \u953  \u950 \u969 \u8134 \u962  \u960 \u949 \u961 \u953 \u960 \u945 \u964 \u942 \u963 \u969 \u956 \u949 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "that we should walk in newness of life."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b Here we encounter the first imperative implied \'97 though stated as purpose.
\f2\b0  The believer's burial with Christ has a stated purpose: that we should walk in newness of life. The indicative serves a practical end.\

\f0\b The walk is grounded in the resurrection
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "like as Christ was raised up from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in newness of life."
\f2\i0  Our walk corresponds to His resurrection. As He was raised to a new mode of life, so we walk in a new mode of life.\

\f0\b (5)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u963 \u973 \u956 \u966 \u965 \u964 \u959 \u953  \u947 \u949 \u947 \u972 \u957 \u945 \u956 \u949 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "we have become grown-together / united / planted together."
\f2\i0  The word 
\f0\b \uc0\u963 \u973 \u956 \u966 \u965 \u964 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  is striking \'97 it means 
\f1\i "grown together"
\f2\i0  (as a graft grows together with its stock). 
\f0\b The believer is grown-together with Christ \'97 in the likeness of His death, and also in the likeness of His resurrection.
\f2\b0 \
This is the indicative reality: we are organically united with Christ in both His death and His resurrection. The resurrection-union is as certain as the death-union.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b ROMANS 6:6-7 \'97 THE OLD MAN CRUCIFIED
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "Knowing this, that our old man is crucified with him, that the body of sin might be destroyed, that henceforth we should not serve sin. For he that is dead, is freed from sin."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of supreme importance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u964 \u959 \u8166 \u964 \u959  \u947 \u953 \u957 \u974 \u963 \u954 \u959 \u957 \u964 \u949 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "knowing this."
\f2\i0  Again, the appeal to knowledge. The indicative must be known before it can be lived.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u960 \u945 \u955 \u945 \u953 \u8056 \u962  \u7969 \u956 \u8182 \u957  \u7940 \u957 \u952 \u961 \u969 \u960 \u959 \u962  \u963 \u965 \u957 \u949 \u963 \u964 \u945 \u965 \u961 \u974 \u952 \u951 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "our old man was crucified together [with Him]."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b The "old man"
\f2\b0  (\uc0\u8001  \u960 \u945 \u955 \u945 \u953 \u8056 \u962  \u7940 \u957 \u952 \u961 \u969 \u960 \u959 \u962 ) is the person we were in Adam \'97 the sinner, the rebel, the bondservant of sin, the one under condemnation. 
\f0\b That person was crucified with Christ.
\f2\b0  The verb is aorist passive \'97 a definite past action done to us by the Father at the cross.\

\f0\b This is one of the most liberating truths in all Scripture.
\f2\b0  The person who lived before conversion \'97 who was a slave to sin, dominated by the flesh, alienated from God \'97 
\f0\b does not exist anymore.
\f2\b0  He was crucified. The believer is not the old man trying to be good; the believer is 
\f0\b a new man
\f2\b0  in whom 
\f0\b the old man no longer lives.
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b (3) The purpose:
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7989 \u957 \u945  \u954 \u945 \u964 \u945 \u961 \u947 \u951 \u952 \u8135  \u964 \u8056  \u963 \u8182 \u956 \u945  \u964 \u8134 \u962  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u945 \u962 , \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u956 \u951 \u954 \u941 \u964 \u953  \u948 \u959 \u965 \u955 \u949 \u973 \u949 \u953 \u957  \u7969 \u956 \u8118 \u962  \u964 \u8135  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u8115 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "that the body of sin might be rendered powerless, that we should no longer serve sin."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b \uc0\u954 \u945 \u964 \u945 \u961 \u947 \u941 \u969 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "to render inoperative, to nullify, to deprive of power."
\f2\i0  The body of sin (the entire structure of sinful patterns, habits, and identity that ruled the old man) 
\f0\b has been rendered powerless
\f2\b0  by the crucifixion of the old man with Christ.\

\f0\b The result:
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u964 \u959 \u8166  \u956 \u951 \u954 \u941 \u964 \u953  \u948 \u959 \u965 \u955 \u949 \u973 \u949 \u953 \u957  \u7969 \u956 \u8118 \u962  \u964 \u8135  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u8115 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "that we should no longer be slaves to sin."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b This is the foundation of Christian freedom from sin's dominion.
\f2\b0  Not that sin is gone (we will see it can still be yielded to), but that 
\f0\b the slavery is broken.
\f2\b0  The old chains have been severed. We are no longer compelled to obey sin's commands. We are now free to obey righteousness.\

\f0\b (4)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u947 \u8048 \u961  \u7936 \u960 \u959 \u952 \u945 \u957 \u8060 \u957  \u948 \u949 \u948 \u953 \u954 \u945 \u943 \u969 \u964 \u945 \u953  \u7936 \u960 \u8056  \u964 \u8134 \u962  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u945 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "For he who has died has been justified / set free from sin."
\f2\i0 \
A legal principle: death pays sin's claim and ends sin's jurisdiction. A dead man cannot be summoned to obey his old master. The believer, having died with Christ, 
\f0\b has been judicially set free
\f2\b0  from sin's claim. The court has no jurisdiction over a corpse.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b ROMANS 6:8-10 \'97 LIVING WITH HIM
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "Wherefore, if we be dead with Christ, we believe that we shall live also with him, Knowing that Christ being raised from the dead, dieth no more: death hath no more dominion over him. For in that he died, he died once to sin but in that he liveth, he liveth to God."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u949 \u7984  \u948 \u8050  \u7936 \u960 \u949 \u952 \u940 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u963 \u8058 \u957  \u935 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u8183 , \u960 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u949 \u973 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u8005 \u964 \u953  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u963 \u965 \u950 \u942 \u963 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "If we died with Christ, we believe we shall also live with Him."
\f2\i0 \
The death-union guarantees the life-union. Paul presents this as an article of faith \'97 
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u949 \u973 \u959 \u956 \u949 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 we 
\f1\i believe
\f2\i0  this. The believer's confidence in his new life with Christ is grounded in his death with Christ.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u935 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u8056 \u962  \u7952 \u947 \u949 \u961 \u952 \u949 \u8054 \u962  \u7952 \u954  \u957 \u949 \u954 \u961 \u8182 \u957  \u959 \u8016 \u954 \u941 \u964 \u953  \u7936 \u960 \u959 \u952 \u957 \u8132 \u963 \u954 \u949 \u953 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "Christ, having been raised from the dead, dies no more."
\f2\i0 \
The risen Christ is in a 
\f0\b once-for-all
\f2\b0  state. He will never die again. Death has no more dominion over Him. 
\f0\b And we are in Him.
\f2\b0  Therefore, the believer's new life, like Christ's, is one over which death has no more dominion. Sin cannot kill us again; we are alive forever in Him.\

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8003  \u7936 \u960 \u941 \u952 \u945 \u957 \u949 \u957  \u964 \u8135  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u8115  \u7936 \u960 \u941 \u952 \u945 \u957 \u949 \u957  \u7952 \u966 \u940 \u960 \u945 \u958 \'b7 \u8003  \u948 \u8050  \u950 \u8135  \u950 \u8135  \u964 \u8183  \u920 \u949 \u8183 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "What He died, He died to sin once for all; what He lives, He lives to God."
\f2\i0 \
Christ's death was a death 
\f0\b to sin
\f2\b0  \'97 not that He sinned, but that He died in relation to sin (bearing it, exhausting its claim, ending its power over Him as our representative). Christ's life now is 
\f0\b a life unto God
\f2\b0  \'97 entirely oriented to the Father.\

\f0\b This is the pattern for the believer.
\f2\b0  As Christ died once for all to sin and now lives unto God, so we, in Him, died once for all to sin and now live unto God. The pattern of His existence is the pattern of ours.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b ROMANS 6:11 \'97 THE FIRST IMPERATIVE: RECKON!
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "Likewise think ye also, that ye are dead to sin, but are alive to God in Jesus Christ our Lord."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of supreme importance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Here is the first explicit imperative of the chapter \'97 and it is breathtakingly precise.
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u959 \u8021 \u964 \u969 \u962  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8017 \u956 \u949 \u8150 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "so also you."
\f2\i0  The pattern that has just been established for Christ (died to sin, lives to God) is now applied to the believer.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u955 \u959 \u947 \u943 \u950 \u949 \u963 \u952 \u949 
\f2\b0  \'97 present imperative \'97 
\f1\i "reckon, count, calculate, consider."
\f2\i0  This is a continuing, present action. 
\f0\b Keep on reckoning yourselves.
\f2\b0 \
The word 
\f0\b \uc0\u955 \u959 \u947 \u943 \u950 \u959 \u956 \u945 \u953 
\f2\b0  is a bookkeeping term. It means 
\f1\i to make a careful calculation and act on it as true.
\f2\i0  The believer is commanded to 
\f0\b add up the facts of his identification with Christ
\f2\b0  and treat the resulting reality as the operative truth of his life.\

\f0\b The reckoning does not create the reality.
\f2\b0  The reality is already true (vv. 1-10 have established it). The reckoning is the believer's daily, moment-by-moment 
\f0\b agreement
\f2\b0  with the reality the Father has already established.\

\f0\b (3) Two facts to be reckoned:
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b \uc0\u957 \u949 \u954 \u961 \u959 \u8058 \u962  \u956 \u8050 \u957  \u964 \u8135  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u8115 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "dead to sin."
\f2\i0  The believer is to count himself as dead to sin. When sin makes its claim \'97 "obey me, you have always obeyed me" \'97 the believer says: 
\f1\i "You speak to a dead man. I am dead to your claims."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b \uc0\u950 \u8182 \u957 \u964 \u945 \u962  \u948 \u8050  \u964 \u8183  \u920 \u949 \u8183  \u7952 \u957  \u935 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u8183  \u7992 \u951 \u963 \u959 \u8166 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "alive to God in Christ Jesus."
\f2\i0  The believer is to count himself as alive \'97 to the Father \'97 in Christ Jesus. 
\f0\b The new identity is Godward.
\f2\b0  The orientation of the new life is toward the Father, through the Son.\

\f0\b (4) This is the heart of the indicative-to-imperative pattern.
\f2\b0  The reality has been established by the Father at the cross and resurrection. The believer's task is to 
\f1\i reckon it true
\f2\i0  and walk accordingly.\
This is 
\f0\b faith applied to identity.
\f2\b0  It is not pretending; it is agreeing with the truth. The Father reckons us dead with Christ \'97 we reckon ourselves dead with Christ. Our reckoning aligns with His.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b ROMANS 6:12-13 \'97 THE PRACTICAL IMPERATIVES
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "Let not sin reign therefore in your mortal body, that ye should obey it in the lusts thereof: Neither give ye your members, as weapons of unrighteousness unto sin: but give yourselves unto God, as they that are alive from the dead, and give your members as weapons of righteousness unto God."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of decisive importance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Now the imperatives multiply.
\f2\b0  Note the careful logic: because v. 11 has been reckoned (the indicative agreed to), now the practical commands follow.\

\f0\b (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u924 \u8052  \u959 \u8022 \u957  \u946 \u945 \u963 \u953 \u955 \u949 \u965 \u941 \u964 \u969  \u7969  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u945 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "Let sin not reign."
\f2\i0  Present imperative \'97 a continuing prohibition. 
\f0\b Stop letting sin reign. Do not allow it to take the throne.
\f2\b0 \
The verb is 
\f0\b \uc0\u946 \u945 \u963 \u953 \u955 \u949 \u973 \u969 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i to reign as king.
\f2\i0  Sin 
\f0\b wants to reign.
\f2\b0  Sin's ambition is dominion. 
\f0\b And the believer's task is to refuse it the throne.
\f2\b0 \
Notice: Paul does not say 
\f1\i "sin cannot be present in you."
\f2\i0  He says 
\f1\i "do not let it reign."
\f2\i0  Sin may still attempt to assert itself; the question is whether you grant it sovereignty. The believer has the standing to refuse \'97 because he died to sin's slavery (v. 6).\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u957  \u964 \u8183  \u952 \u957 \u951 \u964 \u8183  \u8017 \u956 \u8182 \u957  \u963 \u974 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u953 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "in your mortal body."
\f2\i0  This is honest. The body remains mortal \'97 still subject to its passions, its appetites, its weariness, its corruption. The body has not yet been redeemed (Rom 8:23). But sin's dominion in the body is to be refused.\

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u949 \u7984 \u962  \u964 \u8056  \u8017 \u960 \u945 \u954 \u959 \u973 \u949 \u953 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8135  \u964 \u945 \u8150 \u962  \u7952 \u960 \u953 \u952 \u965 \u956 \u943 \u945 \u953 \u962  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "so as to obey it in its desires."
\f2\i0  Sin reigns through the body's desires when we obey them. To refuse sin's reign is to refuse to obey the body's sinful desires.\

\f0\b (4)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u956 \u951 \u948 \u8050  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u940 \u957 \u949 \u964 \u949  \u964 \u8048  \u956 \u941 \u955 \u951  \u8017 \u956 \u8182 \u957  \u8005 \u960 \u955 \u945  \u7936 \u948 \u953 \u954 \u943 \u945 \u962  \u964 \u8135  \u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u8115 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "nor present your members as weapons of unrighteousness to sin."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b \uc0\u8005 \u960 \u955 \u945 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i weapons / instruments.
\f2\i0  The body's members (hands, feet, eyes, tongue, mind) are tools that can be used as 
\f0\b weapons
\f2\b0  \'97 for sin or for righteousness. The believer is commanded 
\f0\b not to make his members available to sin as weapons against God and against righteousness.
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b (5)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7936 \u955 \u955 \u8048  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u963 \u964 \u942 \u963 \u945 \u964 \u949  \u7953 \u945 \u965 \u964 \u959 \u8058 \u962  \u964 \u8183  \u920 \u949 \u8183 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "but present yourselves to God."
\f2\i0 \
Now the 
\f0\b positive command:
\f2\b0  present yourselves 
\f0\b to God.
\f2\b0  The verb is 
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u945 \u961 \u943 \u963 \u964 \u951 \u956 \u953 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i to present, to hand over, to place at someone's disposal.
\f2\i0  The aorist tense here suggests a definitive presentation \'97 make this transfer.\

\f0\b \uc0\u8033 \u963 \u949 \u8054  \u7952 \u954  \u957 \u949 \u954 \u961 \u8182 \u957  \u950 \u8182 \u957 \u964 \u945 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "as those alive from the dead."
\f2\i0  The basis of the self-presentation: 
\f0\b you are alive from the dead.
\f2\b0  Your reckoning of vv. 11 informs your presentation of vv. 13.\

\f0\b (6)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u964 \u8048  \u956 \u941 \u955 \u951  \u8017 \u956 \u8182 \u957  \u8005 \u960 \u955 \u945  \u948 \u953 \u954 \u945 \u953 \u959 \u963 \u973 \u957 \u951 \u962  \u964 \u8183  \u920 \u949 \u8183 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "and your members as weapons of righteousness to God."
\f2\i0 \
The same members that were weapons of unrighteousness are now presented as 
\f0\b weapons of righteousness to God.
\f2\b0  The hands that took are now hands that give. The tongue that lied is now the tongue that speaks truth. The mind that imagined evil is now the mind that meditates on what is true, honorable, just, pure, lovely, and of good report (cf. Phil 4:8).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b ROMANS 6:14 \'97 THE PROMISE THAT SUSTAINS THE COMMANDS
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "For sin shall not have dominion over you: for ye are not under the Law, but under grace."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of foundational importance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7937 \u956 \u945 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u945  \u947 \u8048 \u961  \u8017 \u956 \u8182 \u957  \u959 \u8016  \u954 \u965 \u961 \u953 \u949 \u973 \u963 \u949 \u953 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "For sin shall not have dominion over you."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b This is a promise, not merely a command.
\f2\b0  Future indicative \'97 
\f0\b shall not.
\f2\b0  Paul declares that sin 
\f0\b will not
\f2\b0  rule over the believer.\
But notice: this comes right after the commands of vv. 12-13. The order is profound:\
- The believer is commanded to refuse sin's reign (v. 12).\
- The believer is commanded to present himself to God (v. 13).\
- And then: sin 
\f0\b shall not
\f2\b0  have dominion (v. 14).\

\f0\b The promise is grounded in the obedience to the command \'97 and the obedience to the command is grounded in the indicative reality of co-crucifixion and resurrection.
\f2\b0  The Christian life flows like this:\
1. 
\f0\b What is true
\f2\b0  (indicative \'97 vv. 1-10).\
2. 
\f0\b What we reckon
\f2\b0  (v. 11).\
3. 
\f0\b How we act
\f2\b0  (vv. 12-13).\
4. 
\f0\b What is then experientially real
\f2\b0  (v. 14).\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u959 \u8016  \u947 \u940 \u961  \u7952 \u963 \u964 \u949  \u8017 \u960 \u8056  \u957 \u972 \u956 \u959 \u957  \u7936 \u955 \u955 \u8048  \u8017 \u960 \u8056  \u967 \u940 \u961 \u953 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "For you are not under law but under grace."
\f2\i0 \
This is the foundation of the promise. Under the law, sin had power because the law condemned the sinner and provoked the flesh (Rom 7:8-11). Under grace, the believer stands in the favor of God, possessing the righteousness of Christ, indwelt by the Spirit, free from condemnation. 
\f0\b Sin cannot rule a person who stands in grace
\f2\b0  \'97 for grace has broken sin's grip and provided the resources for victory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b ROMANS 6:15-23 \'97 THE SLAVERY MOTIF
\f2\b0 \
The chapter continues with Paul addressing a second objection \'97 
\f1\i "Shall we sin because we are not under law but under grace?"
\f2\i0  \'97 with a parallel structure: indicative, then imperative.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Romans 6:15-18 (selections):\
1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "What then? shall we sin, because we are not under the Law, but under grace? God forbid. Know ye not, that to whomsoever ye give yourselves as servants to obey, his servants ye are to whom ye obey... But God be thanked, that ye have been the servants of sin, but ye have obeyed from the heart unto the form of the doctrine, whereunto ye were delivered. Being then made free from sin, ye are made the servants of righteousness."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Paul presents two slaveries: slavery to sin (which leads to death) and slavery to righteousness/God (which leads to sanctification and eternal life). The believer has been 
\f0\b set free
\f2\b0  from the first and 
\f0\b made a slave
\f2\b0  to the second.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 The indicative:
\f2\b0  You 
\f0\b were
\f2\b0  slaves of sin; you 
\f0\b have been
\f2\b0  set free; you 
\f0\b are now
\f2\b0  slaves of righteousness (v. 18). These are settled facts.\

\f0\b The imperative implied:
\f2\b0  Therefore present your members to righteousness, just as before you presented them to sin (v. 19).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 # 
\f0\b ROMANS 6:23 \'97 THE CONCLUDING TRUTH
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f1\i\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 "For the wages of sin is death: but the gift of God is eternal life, through Jesus Christ our Lord."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The chapter closes with the great contrast. Sin 
\f0\b pays wages
\f2\b0  \'97 and its wage is death. God 
\f0\b gives
\f2\b0  \'97 and His gift is eternal life. Sin earns; grace gives. The two paths are utterly different in source and destination.\
And the gift is 
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u957  \u935 \u961 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u8183  \u7992 \u951 \u963 \u959 \u8166  \u964 \u8183  \u922 \u965 \u961 \u943 \u8179  \u7969 \u956 \u8182 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "in Christ Jesus our Lord."
\f2\i0  Once again, the locus of the new life is identification with Christ.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
